  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186097833,
      "id": "961ff0d0-9cef-5a2d-8207-63481c0cc1ab",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1.$ \n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $|\\, \\mathbf{a} \\,|,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{ \\mathbf{a} }{ |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| }\n$$\n\nFor example, if $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,$ then the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)",
      "mathAcademyId": "2906",
      "stepId": "17592186097737",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186096447,
      "id": "e301e3f9-501f-5f63-afac-f53379b62c13",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186096813,
        "title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"
      },
      "markdown": "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4282",
      "stepId": "17592186097738",
      "title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector",
      "workedSolution": "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186096493,
      "id": "957d3735-2041-5bec-9e7c-4c1623dd4899",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186096814,
        "title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"
      },
      "markdown": "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-5181",
      "stepId": "17592186097739",
      "title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram",
      "workedSolution": "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186096448,
      "id": "11ad8934-d3f9-546c-b161-9058d251ac66",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186096815,
        "title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"
      },
      "markdown": "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4283",
      "stepId": "17592186097740",
      "title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum",
      "workedSolution": "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|\\mathbf{a}\\mathbf{+}\\mathbf{b}|}^{2} & ={|\\mathbf{a}|}^{2}+{|\\mathbf{b}|}^{2} \\\\  & ={10}^{2}+{20}^{2} \\\\  & =100+400 \\\\  & =500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
